1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
They're precise and lethal.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:09,000
The military equivalent of a hole-in-one.

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:13,000
But on history's front nine, there were a lot of bogeys.

4
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:16,000
Direct hits, duds and dummies.

5
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:18,000
The secret mission of a lost Kennedy.

6
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:21,000
And the breakthroughs that gave the bomb a brain.

7
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:26,000
Now, Smart Bombs on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:48,031 --> 00:00:50,980
They streak to the spot of a laser.

9
00:00:52,800 --> 00:00:54,560
The crosshairs of a camera.

10
00:00:56,280 --> 00:00:58,720
With a pinpoint of GPS coordinates.

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:04,500
to strike the right spot at the optimum altitude and angle.

12
00:01:04,980 --> 00:01:09,180
In some cases, spread the explosive energy in the right direction,

13
00:01:09,640 --> 00:01:12,480
and in some cases, even do that in a three-dimensional sense.

14
00:01:12,820 --> 00:01:15,820
It hits a place not only at the right point on the ground,

15
00:01:15,960 --> 00:01:18,960
but varies underneath the ground in just the right place.

16
00:01:22,920 --> 00:01:26,060
Typically, smart bombs have no propulsion systems of their own,

17
00:01:26,520 --> 00:01:28,880
but are steered in free fall by winglets.

18
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:38,240
The Tomahawk cruise missile, one of the workhorses of the Iraq War, also uses GPS guidance and

19
00:01:38,241 --> 00:01:40,840
television imagery.

20
00:01:40,841 --> 00:01:44,560
But its jet engine makes it a missile, not a smart bomb.

21
00:01:44,561 --> 00:01:48,640
There's one other critical difference.

22
00:01:48,641 --> 00:01:52,840
Cruise missiles cost at least $500,000 each.

23
00:01:52,841 --> 00:01:55,520
A smart bomb, as little as $20,000.

24
00:01:55,521 --> 00:02:06,060
Smart bombs seemed to burst on the scene amid enormous hype during the two wars with Iraq.

25
00:02:06,560 --> 00:02:10,660
But long before the bandwagon of the 1990s, there were smart bombs.

26
00:02:13,560 --> 00:02:20,320
In 1919, Charles Kettering, one of the fathers of precision guidance, had this to say about his brainchild.

27
00:02:20,321 --> 00:02:25,800
What is regarded as one of the most destructive weapons invented during the war

28
00:02:25,801 --> 00:02:29,340
has been placed in the secret archives of the War Department of Washington.

29
00:02:30,420 --> 00:02:34,060
There to remain, it is hoped, even by the inventor, for all times.

30
00:02:35,760 --> 00:02:39,900
Dire words to describe a flimsy biplane whose first flight ended miserably.

31
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:43,480
But this was the world's first smart bomb.

32
00:02:43,481 --> 00:02:53,760
World War I pilots experienced for the first time the stark equation of aerial bombing.

33
00:02:54,620 --> 00:02:56,920
Precision equals proximity to the target.

34
00:02:58,320 --> 00:03:02,580
But the closer you get to the enemy, the better your chances of getting shot out of the sky.

35
00:03:04,200 --> 00:03:09,020
General rule is if the target is worth destroying by us, it's worth protecting by the enemy.

36
00:03:09,380 --> 00:03:12,480
So any target you're going after is probably going to be fairly heavily defended.

37
00:03:13,480 --> 00:03:17,260
The only way to stem the carnage was to start removing the pilot from the equation,

38
00:03:17,700 --> 00:03:19,620
to make a remote-controlled bomb.

39
00:03:21,880 --> 00:03:24,260
This is the so-called aerial torpedo,

40
00:03:25,060 --> 00:03:28,920
a prototype for a guided weapon invented in 1918 for the U.S. Navy.

41
00:03:30,060 --> 00:03:35,140
Not to be outdone, the Army appealed to an inventor and future president of General Motors.

42
00:03:36,100 --> 00:03:37,079
They went to Charles F.

43
00:03:37,080 --> 00:03:40,100
Kettering and asked him if he could develop something similar for them.

44
00:03:41,200 --> 00:03:43,340
Officially still called an aerial torpedo,

45
00:03:43,480 --> 00:03:46,020
The bomb was nicknamed the Kettering Bug.

46
00:03:46,900 --> 00:03:52,540
The bug was a hybrid chemical weapon with a 200-pound payload of high explosives and mustard gas.

47
00:03:53,400 --> 00:03:55,860
A wing and motor assembly was fitted onto the warhead.

48
00:03:57,060 --> 00:03:59,940
For a runway, a short section of dolly track.

49
00:04:05,280 --> 00:04:08,600
After its first crashes, Kettering's bug finally flew.

50
00:04:08,601 --> 00:04:15,600
The fluttering you see is caused by continuous steering input from gyroscopic stabilizers.

51
00:04:15,601 --> 00:04:21,600
Range was controlled by an odometer that counted propeller revolutions.

52
00:04:21,601 --> 00:04:28,600
When it reached a preset distance, up to 75 miles, the ignition was killed and the bomb dove.

53
00:04:28,601 --> 00:04:32,600
But there were a great many bugs in Kettering's bug,

54
00:04:32,601 --> 00:04:37,600
which was unreliable in testing and deployed too late for combat.

55
00:04:37,601 --> 00:04:44,600
Still in great secrecy, work continued on guided bombs.

56
00:04:44,601 --> 00:04:51,600
As two wings gave way to one, in early December 1941, a new kind of flying bomb was tested.

57
00:04:51,601 --> 00:04:56,600
Its first flight, as sorry as the Kettering bugs.

58
00:04:56,601 --> 00:05:06,600
But on December 8, 1941, one day after the Pearl Harbor attack, the so-called controllable bomb went airborne.

59
00:05:06,601 --> 00:05:10,420
an early version of an optical or television bomb.

60
00:05:11,180 --> 00:05:12,000
That's right.

61
00:05:12,540 --> 00:05:17,000
It's 1941, and the military already had a TV camera inside a bomb.

62
00:05:17,880 --> 00:05:21,240
From a chase plane, the bombardiers saw the TV image

63
00:05:21,241 --> 00:05:23,240
and flew the bomb by radio controls,

64
00:05:23,740 --> 00:05:25,920
like a really big remote control plane.

65
00:05:26,160 --> 00:05:28,620
You're essentially sitting inside the bomb

66
00:05:28,621 --> 00:05:31,480
because the TV camera's in the nose, and you're flying the bomb.

67
00:05:32,440 --> 00:05:34,140
But as the conflict deepened,

68
00:05:34,141 --> 00:05:37,560
the U.S. military thought it had the answer to precision bombing.

69
00:05:38,240 --> 00:05:39,940
And it wasn't the controllable bomb.

70
00:05:43,840 --> 00:05:48,340
In order not to bomb churches, habitations,

71
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:52,060
and only to hit military targets,

72
00:05:53,400 --> 00:05:57,360
an instrument of this nature was vital.

73
00:05:58,240 --> 00:06:01,360
The bomb site is just this instrument.

74
00:06:04,140 --> 00:06:10,760
The famed Norton Mark 15 bombsite aimed a bomb drop while taking over a plane's controls during the approach.

75
00:06:17,980 --> 00:06:22,640
Call these dumb bombs, and you'll have to answer to a lot of B-17 bombardiers.

76
00:06:24,560 --> 00:06:26,060
The bombs weren't so dumb.

77
00:06:26,480 --> 00:06:29,400
The equipment they had, the bomb sites they had were actually pretty good.

78
00:06:29,401 --> 00:06:31,580
under a calm environment,

79
00:06:31,700 --> 00:06:33,140
a single airplane can drop the bombs

80
00:06:33,141 --> 00:06:35,560
generally within 500 feet of a target.

81
00:06:37,460 --> 00:06:38,980
But when you put them in large formations

82
00:06:38,981 --> 00:06:40,240
and throw in bad weather

83
00:06:40,241 --> 00:06:41,640
and people shooting at them,

84
00:06:42,040 --> 00:06:43,700
the typical World War II bomb

85
00:06:43,701 --> 00:06:46,340
missed by more than 1,000 feet from any target.

86
00:06:48,540 --> 00:06:50,120
That's why it would take hundreds

87
00:06:50,121 --> 00:06:51,680
and even thousands of airplanes,

88
00:06:51,840 --> 00:06:53,220
say, to destroy a single factory.

89
00:06:54,860 --> 00:06:57,580
The military term was bombs per acre,

90
00:06:58,380 --> 00:06:59,340
saturation bombing.

91
00:06:59,400 --> 00:07:13,400
The ghastly contest of attrition by saturation bombing was the way of the war.

92
00:07:14,460 --> 00:07:18,160
But each of the great powers also dabbled in precision-guided weapons.

93
00:07:25,060 --> 00:07:29,120
The terror bombers of the age flew a barbaric kind of smart bomb.

94
00:07:29,400 --> 00:07:32,880
kamikazes.

95
00:07:35,240 --> 00:07:37,020
Obviously, it kills the pilot,

96
00:07:37,021 --> 00:07:41,060
but they provide that terminal guidance for an anti-ship missile.

97
00:07:45,460 --> 00:07:48,240
The Germans had a sizable arsenal of guided bombs.

98
00:07:49,020 --> 00:07:52,680
One was the Fritz X, named for the X or cross of its tail,

99
00:07:53,100 --> 00:07:55,700
which could be remotely controlled during the bomb's descent.

100
00:07:55,701 --> 00:08:02,640
It had a flare on the back of it so that the pilot could see where the bomb was,

101
00:08:02,940 --> 00:08:07,480
and then he would steer it with a little toggle switch, just like you fly model airplanes today.

102
00:08:07,780 --> 00:08:12,720
And he would guide that and just superimpose that bright dot over the target and glide it in.

103
00:08:17,860 --> 00:08:20,700
Another German smart bomb was the Henschel 293.

104
00:08:20,701 --> 00:08:27,200
A glide bomb, able to fly great distances as the bombardier sighted its flare into the target.

105
00:08:32,200 --> 00:08:36,700
This American glide bomb is television-guided, like the earlier controllable bomb.

106
00:08:37,200 --> 00:08:39,440
The bulge beneath the bomb houses the TV camera.

107
00:08:39,441 --> 00:08:49,500
the shallow angle of attack

108
00:08:49,501 --> 00:08:51,440
was another advantage to the glide bomb

109
00:08:51,441 --> 00:08:53,420
it skipped the ground

110
00:08:53,421 --> 00:08:54,760
until it found the target

111
00:08:54,761 --> 00:09:03,940
here's the view from the bomb

112
00:09:03,941 --> 00:09:05,840
looking remarkably

113
00:09:05,841 --> 00:09:07,600
like an image from the 1990s

114
00:09:07,601 --> 00:09:10,600
But the year is 1944.

115
00:09:13,600 --> 00:09:17,600
The German V-2 rocket lit a fire under yet another smart bomb project,

116
00:09:17,601 --> 00:09:21,600
one that involved the first son of an ill-starred American family.

117
00:09:23,600 --> 00:09:27,600
By the fall of 1944, the V-2 was striking British cities.

118
00:09:28,600 --> 00:09:31,600
Secretly, an American project codenamed Aphrodite

119
00:09:31,601 --> 00:09:34,600
was underway to destroy the V-2 launch sites.

120
00:09:34,601 --> 00:09:43,300
Worn out bombers, like the B-24 depicted in this period cartoon, were reenlisted, wired

121
00:09:43,301 --> 00:09:49,120
for radio remote control, fitted with television cameras.

122
00:09:49,121 --> 00:09:58,740
The plane was then loaded up with explosives to make the equivalent of a 20,000 pound bomb.

123
00:09:58,741 --> 00:10:01,820
One of the Aphrodite volunteers was Joe Kennedy Jr.

124
00:10:01,821 --> 00:10:03,820
Joseph P. Kennedy Jr. was a Navy pilot.

125
00:10:03,821 --> 00:10:06,040
He had just completed his tour of duty in Europe.

126
00:10:06,680 --> 00:10:08,020
Technically, he could have gone home,

127
00:10:08,300 --> 00:10:11,020
but he volunteered to stay for this highly classified project.

128
00:10:11,480 --> 00:10:14,620
Basically, he had volunteered to be a takeoff pilot.

129
00:10:18,280 --> 00:10:21,180
Pilot and co-pilot were needed to get the bomber airborne,

130
00:10:21,700 --> 00:10:23,460
after which they could parachute to safety.

131
00:10:25,440 --> 00:10:28,880
The pilotless bomber could then be remotely steered over the channel

132
00:10:28,881 --> 00:10:30,260
and into the V-2 sites.

133
00:10:30,261 --> 00:10:36,400
On August 12, 1944, Kennedy took off on mission number three.

134
00:10:36,900 --> 00:10:38,720
Joe Jr.'s mission should have been a really short one.

135
00:10:40,240 --> 00:10:44,500
He and his co-pilot Wilfred Willey really just needed to take this bomber off,

136
00:10:44,600 --> 00:10:47,320
get it into straight and level flight, and then bail out.

137
00:10:47,900 --> 00:10:51,200
While they were still over Britain, there was a premature detonation.

138
00:10:52,020 --> 00:10:54,880
As a secret report said, the plane was, quote,

139
00:10:54,881 --> 00:10:59,020
suddenly enveloped in a large circular ball of flame and white smoke

140
00:10:59,021 --> 00:11:00,740
and disintegrated in the air.

141
00:11:02,500 --> 00:11:05,920
The bodies of Kennedy and his co-pilot were never found.

142
00:11:08,360 --> 00:11:10,020
Aphrodite flew a few more missions,

143
00:11:10,500 --> 00:11:12,180
but never knocked out a V-2 base.

144
00:11:13,200 --> 00:11:16,040
Later, in 1944, the project was scrapped.

145
00:11:17,740 --> 00:11:20,900
Then, the drive towards guided weapons abruptly vanished

146
00:11:20,901 --> 00:11:22,580
in twin mushroom clouds.

147
00:11:25,220 --> 00:11:25,920
Hiroshima.

148
00:11:29,020 --> 00:11:30,020
Nagasaki.

149
00:11:31,580 --> 00:11:34,920
Development of conventional guided munitions ceased.

150
00:11:35,080 --> 00:11:37,300
They ceased all funding for it and all interest in it

151
00:11:37,301 --> 00:11:40,280
because they thought that the nuclear weapons would replace all that

152
00:11:40,281 --> 00:11:41,940
and there would not be any more conventional wars.

153
00:11:50,620 --> 00:11:54,540
But the history of warfare was about to take a few unexpected detours

154
00:11:54,541 --> 00:11:56,900
through places like Korea and Vietnam.

155
00:11:56,901 --> 00:12:00,900
Places where you could never drop a nuke because of the political fallout.

156
00:12:00,901 --> 00:12:05,900
But where you could sure use a bomb precisely guided by a laser beam.

157
00:12:07,900 --> 00:12:11,900
The first battleship sunk by a guided weapon was the Italian ship Roma.

158
00:12:12,900 --> 00:12:16,900
On September 9th, 1943, as she was attempting to defect to the Allies,

159
00:12:16,901 --> 00:12:20,900
the Roma was struck by two Fritz X bombs and blown in half.

160
00:12:21,900 --> 00:12:24,900
Smart Bombs will return on Modern Marvels.

161
00:12:26,900 --> 00:12:30,900
bombs away over North Korea.

162
00:12:32,760 --> 00:12:34,380
Or rather, bomb away.

163
00:12:35,140 --> 00:12:36,800
Because in theory, you just needed one.

164
00:12:37,340 --> 00:12:42,180
The bright red bridge killer, called a Tarzan, all 13,000 pounds worth.

165
00:12:43,880 --> 00:12:47,180
The Tarzan was so big that a B-29 could only hold one bomb.

166
00:12:47,960 --> 00:12:52,120
And even to hold one, it had to have a modified bomb bay where it was half exposed.

167
00:12:54,960 --> 00:12:55,880
What's in a name?

168
00:12:56,660 --> 00:13:00,360
In the case of Tarzan, it's a resume of guided weapons since World War II.

169
00:13:02,600 --> 00:13:11,520
The first precision weapon or radio-controlled weapon that was produced in real quantity during World War II was the Azon, which was an acronym for azimuth only.

170
00:13:13,160 --> 00:13:14,520
Azimuth means horizontal.

171
00:13:15,380 --> 00:13:17,580
An Azon could only be steered left and right.

172
00:13:18,900 --> 00:13:21,720
Late in the war, distance or range control was achieved.

173
00:13:22,600 --> 00:13:27,300
This new class of guided bombs were called Raison's, for range and azimuth only control.

174
00:13:28,480 --> 00:13:34,920
Finally, there was the Tallboy, or British oversized bomb, the bunker buster of its day.

175
00:13:37,740 --> 00:13:45,360
Tarzan stood for Tallboy, Range and Azimuth only bomb, the knockout punch of a Tallboy combined with the pinpoint control of the Raison.

176
00:13:45,361 --> 00:13:51,020
The maneuver capability of that weapon was on the order of about 1,000 feet left or right,

177
00:13:51,480 --> 00:13:57,600
and probably about 1,000 feet long or short of where it would hit if you had put no guidance into it at all.

178
00:13:59,540 --> 00:14:04,160
Like the guided bombs of World War II, a flare ignited as the Tarzan fell.

179
00:14:05,540 --> 00:14:09,400
The bombardier used a joystick to keep the flare superimposed over the target.

180
00:14:11,440 --> 00:14:15,200
But there was a problem with Tarzans and other Korean-era guided bombs.

181
00:14:15,360 --> 00:14:17,560
The boys in the planes just didn't like them.

182
00:14:18,760 --> 00:14:22,620
Air crews really were not enthusiastic about the early precision-guided munitions.

183
00:14:22,940 --> 00:14:26,200
It made them fly a very predictable flight path or bomb run.

184
00:14:26,720 --> 00:14:31,060
The predictability came from maintaining a straight course while the bomb was guided toward its target,

185
00:14:32,240 --> 00:14:36,700
making the plane a sitting duck for anti-aircraft fire for enemy fighters.

186
00:14:36,701 --> 00:14:48,580
from world war ii through korea guided bombs had the stigma of being oddball devices that

187
00:14:48,581 --> 00:14:54,220
few airmen wanted a part of commanders took the view i think that it was just a quirky experiment

188
00:14:54,221 --> 00:14:59,260
and they were really into massive bombing it didn't matter how many airplanes and bombs

189
00:14:59,261 --> 00:15:02,140
there were plenty of them available so you could send what you needed to destroy target

190
00:15:02,141 --> 00:15:10,340
Then came the conflict where indiscriminate bombing was no longer politically acceptable.

191
00:15:11,260 --> 00:15:11,960
Vietnam.

192
00:15:14,080 --> 00:15:16,560
The politics reached all the way into the cockpit.

193
00:15:17,480 --> 00:15:21,100
Air crews flew under restrictive ROEs, or rules of engagement.

194
00:15:21,820 --> 00:15:24,460
A long list of targets that you couldn't bomb.

195
00:15:26,500 --> 00:15:31,040
Contrary to what you would read in the press, we were very careful of how we hit our targets.

196
00:15:31,041 --> 00:15:36,080
We took pride in the fact that we could accomplish our mission exactly as we were told to.

197
00:15:36,640 --> 00:15:40,920
And we were very careful to make sure that we hit our targets, specifically what we were supposed to hit.

198
00:15:42,460 --> 00:15:46,880
But politics wasn't on the minds of the men devising the next generation of smart bombs.

199
00:15:47,840 --> 00:15:52,100
They just wanted to make a weapon that really worked and didn't endanger the pilot.

200
00:15:53,760 --> 00:15:59,940
A World War II ace, Korea jet pilot, and Air Force acquisitions officer named Joe Davis

201
00:15:59,941 --> 00:16:03,518
sparkplugged the project. Well, I was in World War II.

202
00:16:03,519 --> 00:16:06,300
There was a lot of bombing done. We had to

203
00:16:06,301 --> 00:16:10,698
bomb with many, many airplanes to destroy the target.

204
00:16:10,699 --> 00:16:13,520
He, in his way, was an operational

205
00:16:13,521 --> 00:16:18,700
visionary, not a technical visionary, but he had been there, and he knew the problems those kids

206
00:16:18,701 --> 00:16:20,347
were facing in Vietnam.

207
00:16:20,348 --> 00:16:25,360
In 1964, an executive named Glenn Peniston from a little-known company

208
00:16:25,361 --> 00:16:27,560
named Texas Instruments, called on Davis.

209
00:16:28,180 --> 00:16:31,480
He had a prototype of a sensor for a proposed radar-guided bomb.

210
00:16:32,880 --> 00:16:37,200
On the spot, Davis wondered if the sensor could be adapted to seek out a laser beam.

211
00:16:39,680 --> 00:16:42,700
He gave the company a single weekend to work up a proposal.

212
00:16:43,440 --> 00:16:46,200
One engineer who lost his weekend was Weldon Word.

213
00:16:46,620 --> 00:16:52,080
I would say that 60 or 70 percent of the things that we wrote down

214
00:16:52,081 --> 00:16:56,000
and conceived with the slide rule that weekend stayed forever.

215
00:16:58,260 --> 00:16:59,580
The idea was simple.

216
00:17:00,160 --> 00:17:04,020
From a pointer or designator, a laser is focused on a target.

217
00:17:04,920 --> 00:17:08,200
Though invisible to us, to the sensor on the nose of the bomb,

218
00:17:08,600 --> 00:17:10,000
it's like a flashlight in the dark.

219
00:17:11,400 --> 00:17:15,000
The sensor is screwed to the front fuse mount of a general-purpose bomb.

220
00:17:16,160 --> 00:17:20,780
This used what we call a birdie head that would free float in the wind

221
00:17:20,781 --> 00:17:22,380
as the bomb fell to the ground.

222
00:17:22,680 --> 00:17:24,640
That would keep it pointed in the direction

223
00:17:24,641 --> 00:17:26,180
the bomb is actually flying.

224
00:17:26,740 --> 00:17:29,800
In the front is a quadrant detector.

225
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:33,240
The quadrant detector has four laser receivers.

226
00:17:34,200 --> 00:17:36,580
If the laser spot that's on the ground

227
00:17:36,581 --> 00:17:38,820
would go to the top part of the quadrant,

228
00:17:38,980 --> 00:17:41,020
the bomb would then steer in that direction

229
00:17:41,021 --> 00:17:42,880
so that the spot would be in the middle.

230
00:17:44,740 --> 00:17:46,740
To further simplify the design,

231
00:17:46,741 --> 00:17:49,360
the winglets go only hard left or hard right.

232
00:17:50,280 --> 00:17:51,600
Bang-bang steering, it's called,

233
00:17:52,300 --> 00:17:54,800
constantly correcting the bomb's course all the way to the target.

234
00:17:59,880 --> 00:18:02,960
This footage from 1968 is of an advanced test.

235
00:18:04,580 --> 00:18:08,220
Laser-guided bombs had already been proven accurate against stationary targets.

236
00:18:10,640 --> 00:18:14,860
But could one hit a moving target, such as this remote-controlled tank?

237
00:18:14,861 --> 00:18:20,020
The young man tank was set loose

238
00:18:20,021 --> 00:18:23,660
One jet followed it with a laser beam

239
00:18:23,661 --> 00:18:27,520
While the other one released the bomb

240
00:18:27,521 --> 00:18:35,120
Striking within 10 feet was considered to be a direct hit

241
00:18:38,660 --> 00:18:40,220
Davis's biggest challenge though

242
00:18:40,221 --> 00:18:42,980
Might have been guiding the bomb through the Air Force bureaucracy

243
00:18:43,540 --> 00:18:46,840
Now I wind up arguing with the two-star general.

244
00:18:47,860 --> 00:18:50,780
He's telling me it won't work, it's not any good.

245
00:18:51,180 --> 00:18:54,380
And I'm telling them we've already hit the target.

246
00:18:54,520 --> 00:18:57,380
We've got a CEP of 10 to 12 feet.

247
00:18:57,580 --> 00:18:59,500
We already hit a moving tank.

248
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:03,500
And then the three-star general at the end of the table shut him up.

249
00:19:03,501 --> 00:19:13,040
By 1968, the $3,000 weapon was deployed in Vietnam.

250
00:19:13,660 --> 00:19:15,220
This time, the air crews approved.

251
00:19:15,760 --> 00:19:18,160
We used them primarily against pinpoint targets.

252
00:19:18,300 --> 00:19:20,480
Laser bombs were perfect for pinpoint targets,

253
00:19:20,800 --> 00:19:24,100
such as targets like gun emplacements, AAA.

254
00:19:24,760 --> 00:19:27,520
We used them against bulldozers even,

255
00:19:27,640 --> 00:19:30,700
or we'd try to pick the exact point we'd want to cut a road

256
00:19:30,701 --> 00:19:32,740
to stop traffic from flowing.

257
00:19:33,500 --> 00:19:37,820
Air crews called them Zots, a name borrowed from Flash Gordon.

258
00:19:38,680 --> 00:19:41,300
Zot vanished, but one other nickname stuck.

259
00:19:41,960 --> 00:19:44,700
I heard the term smart bomb when I was in that squatter.

260
00:19:44,800 --> 00:19:48,700
We used to call them smart bombs, and the unguided bombs were called dumb bombs.

261
00:19:49,660 --> 00:19:55,160
In a single operation against a bridge in 1972, smart won out over dumb.

262
00:19:55,161 --> 00:20:05,080
This was one of the deadliest airspaces of the Vietnam War

263
00:20:05,081 --> 00:20:09,180
Above the so-called Dragon's Jaw

264
00:20:09,181 --> 00:20:11,080
The Tan Hoa Bridge

265
00:20:11,081 --> 00:20:12,480
Over the Song Ma River

266
00:20:12,481 --> 00:20:18,680
For five years

267
00:20:18,681 --> 00:20:20,780
The steel and concrete reinforced bridge

268
00:20:20,781 --> 00:20:22,640
Had been impervious to conventional bombs

269
00:20:22,641 --> 00:20:24,740
Where they'd flown hundreds of sorties

270
00:20:24,741 --> 00:20:30,200
lost dozens of airplanes and crews trying to destroy this massive iron bridge.

271
00:20:31,160 --> 00:20:35,680
Aircraft strike after aircraft strike went into the area and dropped their dumb bombs.

272
00:20:35,860 --> 00:20:40,560
Unfortunately, most of these bombs drifted in the wind and missed the target.

273
00:20:40,940 --> 00:20:46,140
So there were craters all around the area of the bridge, but the bridge was still standing.

274
00:20:48,360 --> 00:20:52,820
In May 1972, eight planes were sent against the bridge.

275
00:20:53,800 --> 00:20:58,640
Four planes trained lasers on the target, while the other four released their bombs.

276
00:21:01,460 --> 00:21:06,440
Four direct hits, and the dragon's jaw had taken a knockout punch.

277
00:21:12,960 --> 00:21:15,560
Everything up to now had been prehistory.

278
00:21:16,260 --> 00:21:18,880
Other devices had been complex and technology-laden.

279
00:21:18,881 --> 00:21:21,840
But the laser-guided bomb was smart.

280
00:21:22,680 --> 00:21:26,660
I would say that was really the first weapon that worked,

281
00:21:26,720 --> 00:21:30,200
and it worked so well that air crews didn't mind dropping them.

282
00:21:30,240 --> 00:21:32,320
In fact, they were enthusiastic about dropping them.

283
00:21:34,760 --> 00:21:37,480
Still, they were small fry in the big picture.

284
00:21:37,860 --> 00:21:39,880
Politically, Vietnam was an aberration.

285
00:21:40,140 --> 00:21:43,220
It was a small war, and we're still in the heart of the Cold War.

286
00:21:43,400 --> 00:21:45,560
These things aren't going to happen on a regular basis.

287
00:21:47,160 --> 00:21:50,580
But the small war would become all too regular.

288
00:21:51,840 --> 00:21:59,360
giving rise to both a seeing-eye bomb and a GPS bomb that could literally hit what he couldn't see.

289
00:22:01,980 --> 00:22:06,420
During the Vietnam War, the U.S. dropped more than 7 million tons of bombs,

290
00:22:07,200 --> 00:22:10,240
fewer than 25,000 tons of which were laser-guided.

291
00:22:10,900 --> 00:22:12,980
Smart bombs will return on Modern Marvels.

292
00:22:15,600 --> 00:22:21,420
You are the bomb, hunting the enemy and hurtling towards him at thousands of feet per second.

293
00:22:21,840 --> 00:22:32,000
That's the unnerving image you get from an electro-optical bomb.

294
00:22:35,060 --> 00:22:39,320
Optical bombs were a 1960s update of the old TV bombs of World War II.

295
00:22:40,420 --> 00:22:44,700
A camera's in the nose, but the pilot doesn't need to manually steer the bomb in.

296
00:22:45,280 --> 00:22:47,600
He just clicks on the part of the screen he wants to hit.

297
00:22:47,601 --> 00:22:53,840
The bomb itself can lock on to a portion of that TV image and then steer itself to that precise point.

298
00:22:55,500 --> 00:22:58,280
Or, as pilots call it, fire and forget.

299
00:23:01,180 --> 00:23:05,520
This is a 1971 version of the Walleye electro-optical bomb.

300
00:23:06,680 --> 00:23:12,600
They cost $35,000 each and, like your camera, had a lens cap you had to remember to remove.

301
00:23:17,600 --> 00:23:20,760
During flight, a second protective cap is jettisoned.

302
00:23:21,500 --> 00:23:23,120
Now the bomb's eye is wide open.

303
00:23:24,400 --> 00:23:25,520
Target is locked.

304
00:23:27,280 --> 00:23:28,640
Walleye hits the base.

305
00:23:33,780 --> 00:23:38,000
Throughout the Cold War, smart bombs were overshadowed by nuclear weapons.

306
00:23:39,360 --> 00:23:42,900
Then in 1990, the Soviet Union ceased to be.

307
00:23:43,620 --> 00:23:46,840
Gone, too, was the prime reason for all those thousands of nukes.

308
00:23:47,600 --> 00:23:49,840
mad or mutually assured destruction.

309
00:23:53,020 --> 00:23:56,280
One year later, a new generation of superweapons

310
00:23:56,281 --> 00:23:57,980
was ready for their primetime debut.

311
00:24:00,780 --> 00:24:03,960
Of course, Desert Storm was a media event in a lot of cases.

312
00:24:04,100 --> 00:24:07,740
I mean, you'd get a lot of footage from Vietnam and see those things,

313
00:24:07,840 --> 00:24:09,760
but it would be events that happened weeks ago.

314
00:24:09,761 --> 00:24:12,960
During Desert Storm, you're getting things that happened last night.

315
00:24:12,961 --> 00:24:20,400
During the 30 days of aerial bombardment, all the talk was of smart bombs.

316
00:24:24,840 --> 00:24:29,720
Gun camera footage from electro-optical weapons put TV viewers inside the bomb.

317
00:24:33,720 --> 00:24:39,360
Laser-guided bombs were even more accurate, striking an average of 3 meters away from the crosshairs.

318
00:24:39,361 --> 00:24:43,380
The word plink entered the vocabulary

319
00:24:43,381 --> 00:24:45,920
As in tank plinking

320
00:24:45,921 --> 00:24:48,380
Or smart bombs destroyed enemy vehicles

321
00:24:48,381 --> 00:24:55,740
Another euphemism, collateral damage, gained currency

322
00:24:55,741 --> 00:24:59,740
The number of civilians or collateral dead and injured

323
00:24:59,741 --> 00:25:03,540
Were fewer than the saturation bombing campaigns of previous wars

324
00:25:03,541 --> 00:25:10,680
but when the 30 days were over

325
00:25:10,681 --> 00:25:13,120
the numbers didn't quite stack up to the hype

326
00:25:13,121 --> 00:25:16,240
the military had relied mostly on the conventional

327
00:25:16,241 --> 00:25:17,620
or dumb arsenal

328
00:25:17,621 --> 00:25:20,300
just 17% of bombs dropped

329
00:25:20,301 --> 00:25:21,100
were smart

330
00:25:21,101 --> 00:25:24,120
the primary precision guided munitions

331
00:25:24,121 --> 00:25:26,060
delivery platform was the F-111

332
00:25:26,061 --> 00:25:27,620
and there was only one wing of those

333
00:25:27,621 --> 00:25:28,760
that had that capability

334
00:25:28,761 --> 00:25:31,560
so you're still looking at a limited force

335
00:25:31,561 --> 00:25:32,560
to be able to deliver them

336
00:25:32,561 --> 00:25:35,800
but they were able to destroy a disproportionate number of targets

337
00:25:35,801 --> 00:25:38,360
because of the precision guidance that they had.

338
00:25:39,340 --> 00:25:43,440
One thing the Desert Storm proved was what smart bombs couldn't do.

339
00:25:44,320 --> 00:25:47,720
Laser-guided bombs were only effective in almost perfect conditions.

340
00:25:47,721 --> 00:25:51,120
The laser-guided bombs, of course, require a laser beam

341
00:25:51,121 --> 00:25:53,760
to go all the way from the airplane to the target.

342
00:25:54,320 --> 00:25:56,380
That requires it to be line of sight.

343
00:25:57,720 --> 00:26:02,180
Clouds or even battlefield smoke could disrupt line of sight, disabling the lasers.

344
00:26:02,560 --> 00:26:07,620
Electro-optical bombs could dive beneath the clouds or smoke to reveal a target,

345
00:26:08,160 --> 00:26:09,520
but they were useless at night.

346
00:26:10,420 --> 00:26:14,660
General Merrill McPeak, for one, saw an urgent need for a new kind of smart bomb.

347
00:26:15,060 --> 00:26:19,040
In this handwritten note, the Air Force chief of staff groped for a solution.

348
00:26:19,700 --> 00:26:21,780
And he just wrote a note that said,

349
00:26:21,781 --> 00:26:24,440
I need an all-weather precision-guided ammunition.

350
00:26:24,640 --> 00:26:27,000
And there was a question on it that maybe it would be radar-guided.

351
00:26:27,080 --> 00:26:30,460
He didn't know what he wanted, but he knew he wanted something different from what he had.

352
00:26:30,461 --> 00:26:35,160
In a few short years, McPeak got what he wanted.

353
00:26:35,560 --> 00:26:39,620
And it wasn't radar, but GPS, Global Positioning System.

354
00:26:39,621 --> 00:26:44,760
The solution used the same GPS system that your rich cousin has in his Lexus

355
00:26:44,761 --> 00:26:46,980
and your rich uncle has in his yacht.

356
00:26:47,440 --> 00:26:54,420
It's a system that uses satellites to transmit your location on the face of the Earth

357
00:26:54,421 --> 00:26:57,200
in terms of latitude, longitude, and altitude.

358
00:26:57,201 --> 00:27:01,720
Four satellites are used to determine a GPS location.

359
00:27:02,620 --> 00:27:08,580
Each satellite precisely computes its distance from a place in terms of how long it takes for a radio signal to reach it.

360
00:27:09,560 --> 00:27:13,880
There's a single point in three-dimensional space where the distances will intersect.

361
00:27:14,580 --> 00:27:15,900
That's the GPS location.

362
00:27:17,120 --> 00:27:20,140
The new satellite-guided bomb was named JDAM.

363
00:27:20,900 --> 00:27:24,400
JDAM stands for Joint Direct Attack Munition.

364
00:27:24,401 --> 00:27:27,240
Joint means that it's used by more than one service.

365
00:27:27,480 --> 00:27:30,260
In this case, it's used by the Air Force, the Navy, and the Marine Corps.

366
00:27:30,700 --> 00:27:33,120
And direct attack means it's really a short-range munition.

367
00:27:33,240 --> 00:27:34,800
It's not a long-range powered missile.

368
00:27:36,240 --> 00:27:39,400
A JDAM is usually dropped within 15 miles of a target.

369
00:27:41,760 --> 00:27:44,340
GPS tells the bomb where it is and where it's going.

370
00:27:45,280 --> 00:27:47,880
How it actually gets there is the job of its guidance system,

371
00:27:48,400 --> 00:27:49,840
called inertial navigation,

372
00:27:50,160 --> 00:27:52,600
which tracks the movement of the bomb through space.

373
00:27:52,601 --> 00:28:01,520
This inertial navigation system can sense when it moves up, when it moves down, when it rotates.

374
00:28:02,240 --> 00:28:09,060
And every time it moves in any direction, it tells the computer what direction and how much it moved.

375
00:28:09,500 --> 00:28:15,620
So if it knows where it started, any time it moves, it can tell you where it is now.

376
00:28:16,940 --> 00:28:19,920
Inertial navigation by itself is reasonably accurate.

377
00:28:20,620 --> 00:28:23,100
With GPS assist, it's precise.

378
00:28:23,520 --> 00:28:26,400
The inertial navigation system is a precise clock.

379
00:28:26,760 --> 00:28:32,720
The GPS is used to calibrate the clock over and over again to keep it as precise as possible.

380
00:28:34,940 --> 00:28:39,060
They did grossly undershoot one target, that of cost.

381
00:28:39,720 --> 00:28:42,480
The original cost ceiling was set at $40,000.

382
00:28:43,080 --> 00:28:45,420
The actual production figure was half that.

383
00:28:46,340 --> 00:28:49,780
One of the main advantages of JDAM is that it's a very low-cost weapon.

384
00:28:49,920 --> 00:28:53,060
So you don't have to worry about spending a million dollars on a silver bullet.

385
00:28:53,200 --> 00:28:58,600
It's $20,000 each, and the Air Force and the Navy know that they can use a lot of them if they need to.

386
00:29:01,220 --> 00:29:02,460
Among the disadvantages?

387
00:29:03,360 --> 00:29:08,600
With a GPS bomb, you can only hit stationary targets, buildings, not vehicles.

388
00:29:10,420 --> 00:29:12,300
But other bombs could blink tanks.

389
00:29:14,460 --> 00:29:17,700
With the JDAM, the Precision Dream Team was complete.

390
00:29:17,701 --> 00:29:21,460
laser guidance for greatest precision

391
00:29:21,461 --> 00:29:25,100
GPS assist for all weather day and night bombing

392
00:29:25,101 --> 00:29:29,160
electro-optics for the flexibility of flying the bomb

393
00:29:29,161 --> 00:29:32,640
throughout the 90s the arsenal was rolled out

394
00:29:32,641 --> 00:29:35,420
so we see in 91 in the Gulf War

395
00:29:35,421 --> 00:29:39,560
then in 99 in Kosovo

396
00:29:39,561 --> 00:29:42,020
2002 in Afghanistan

397
00:29:42,021 --> 00:29:44,040
and then 2003 in Iraq

398
00:29:44,041 --> 00:29:47,360
this ramping up of the employment of precision guided munitions

399
00:29:47,361 --> 00:29:55,360
66% of all bombs dropped in the second Iraq war were precision guided.

400
00:29:55,361 --> 00:30:00,360
Smart bombs had clearly affected a revolution in arms.

401
00:30:00,361 --> 00:30:06,360
But critics were asking, what about the news accounts of weapons gone tragically off course?

402
00:30:06,361 --> 00:30:12,360
How can smart bombs go dumb?

403
00:30:12,361 --> 00:30:19,260
One smart bomb was entirely designed, built, and deployed during the 43-day duration of the Gulf War.

404
00:30:19,900 --> 00:30:26,340
The laser-guided GBU-28 Bunker Buster had a body improvised from surplus howitzer barrels.

405
00:30:27,300 --> 00:30:29,360
Smart bombs will return on Modern Marvels.

406
00:30:32,220 --> 00:30:34,980
Precision-guided bombs usually live up to the name.

407
00:30:36,020 --> 00:30:41,860
But why do a persistent few go off course, sometimes hitting precisely the wrong targets?

408
00:30:42,360 --> 00:30:46,880
Check out this crater.

409
00:30:47,600 --> 00:30:50,860
Mark Garlasco has seen both sides of the precision arsenal.

410
00:30:51,860 --> 00:30:56,360
He's a former intelligence officer who left the Pentagon to become a civilian watchdog.

411
00:30:56,740 --> 00:30:58,060
It was kind of strange for me.

412
00:30:58,660 --> 00:31:02,840
You know, I had been one of the folks involved in actually determining the aim points,

413
00:31:03,160 --> 00:31:05,080
and here I was standing in front of these buildings,

414
00:31:05,520 --> 00:31:10,240
working on a mission for Human Rights Watch to assess the conduct of the war.

415
00:31:10,241 --> 00:31:12,800
Ah, there's the telecommunications center.

416
00:31:14,280 --> 00:31:16,440
5,000 pounder right down the belly.

417
00:31:17,320 --> 00:31:20,540
So I knew what the aim points were, and I was able to look at buildings and say,

418
00:31:20,640 --> 00:31:25,980
gosh, you know, in this case, each one of these weapons struck exactly where they were supposed to.

419
00:31:26,360 --> 00:31:30,860
Or in some cases, you know, hey, there was one miss, or this one was off a few hundred yards.

420
00:31:31,160 --> 00:31:34,260
This is the Nazaria Bath Party headquarters.

421
00:31:35,380 --> 00:31:38,740
As you can see, the facility is completely destroyed.

422
00:31:40,240 --> 00:31:43,340
According to the Pentagon, smart bombs are 80% accurate.

423
00:31:45,200 --> 00:31:47,260
One in five is a miss.

424
00:31:50,840 --> 00:31:54,020
Of these, a small percentage just don't work.

425
00:31:55,360 --> 00:31:59,460
These are mechanical devices, and all mechanical devices have a certain error.

426
00:31:59,820 --> 00:32:03,660
A JDAM, for example, has about a 2% mechanical error.

427
00:32:04,100 --> 00:32:07,860
Just because of the nature of mechanical devices, it will fail 2% of the time.

428
00:32:08,560 --> 00:32:10,100
Then you also have human error.

429
00:32:10,420 --> 00:32:14,580
You've got to have a person inputting the coordinates for where the bomb is going to be dropped.

430
00:32:15,060 --> 00:32:17,800
And if they make a mistake, people will die.

431
00:32:18,880 --> 00:32:22,920
There are also intelligence errors, despite a rigorous system of targeting.

432
00:32:23,700 --> 00:32:26,460
When I was in the Pentagon, intelligence drove targeting.

433
00:32:26,880 --> 00:32:29,180
And we would take a look at what the objective was.

434
00:32:29,580 --> 00:32:32,760
And we would feed targets to the warfighter.

435
00:32:33,340 --> 00:32:34,980
There would be a review of those targets.

436
00:32:35,400 --> 00:32:39,440
Lawyers would look at it for compliance with international humanitarian law.

437
00:32:40,140 --> 00:32:43,200
And then the warfighter would push it back to us.

438
00:32:43,460 --> 00:32:45,840
And say, these are the ones that we still have on the target list.

439
00:32:46,660 --> 00:32:51,560
With potentially hundreds or thousands of targets on that list, there will be errors.

440
00:32:54,440 --> 00:32:56,480
Kosovo, May 1999.

441
00:32:57,560 --> 00:33:03,200
CIA targeteers choose a building in downtown Belgrade, they believe to be a weapons depot,

442
00:33:03,201 --> 00:33:08,700
and dispatch a B-2 bomber with five JDAM GPS-guided weapons to strike it.

443
00:33:09,120 --> 00:33:09,900
Were they accurate?

444
00:33:10,980 --> 00:33:12,060
Very accurate.

445
00:33:12,500 --> 00:33:13,720
All five struck the building.

446
00:33:14,200 --> 00:33:15,880
All five struck their aim points.

447
00:33:16,560 --> 00:33:19,400
The problem is, the building wasn't what they thought it was.

448
00:33:21,920 --> 00:33:24,380
The building was the Chinese embassy.

449
00:33:25,140 --> 00:33:28,000
There were two dozen casualties, four killed,

450
00:33:28,001 --> 00:33:31,120
and the United States had a diplomatic crisis on its hands.

451
00:33:31,980 --> 00:33:36,300
The official explanation was that targeteers were working from an older map of the city.

452
00:33:37,160 --> 00:33:39,640
The Chinese embassy had moved years earlier.

453
00:33:42,100 --> 00:33:45,900
In Iraqi freedom, the problem wasn't that we didn't have the right address.

454
00:33:46,400 --> 00:33:48,400
It was that often, no one was home.

455
00:33:49,120 --> 00:33:52,000
One of the issues is, a lot of the buildings were empty.

456
00:33:52,800 --> 00:33:55,380
When the bombings took place at the beginning of the war,

457
00:33:55,740 --> 00:33:59,480
the Iraqis had plenty of lead time, and they knew that the war was coming.

458
00:34:00,020 --> 00:34:01,800
So this is one of Saddam's bunkers.

459
00:34:03,520 --> 00:34:07,620
You know, when I was in the Pentagon, you're doing the best job that you can with the information that you have.

460
00:34:07,621 --> 00:34:13,560
And you have to understand that the adversary is doing his very best to deny the information from you.

461
00:34:14,060 --> 00:34:18,140
And so you're trying to put together a puzzle from thousands of miles away.

462
00:34:18,640 --> 00:34:21,060
Or sometimes from just a hilltop away.

463
00:34:21,061 --> 00:34:29,800
Like Mark Garlasco, Green Beret Captain Jason Amerine has also seen both sides of smart bombs.

464
00:34:32,160 --> 00:34:37,120
Saved by laser bombs one day, struck by a 2,000-pound J-DAM the next,

465
00:34:37,720 --> 00:34:41,460
in what's been called the worst friendly fire incident of the Afghanistan war.

466
00:34:42,200 --> 00:34:45,380
In 2001, after the World Trade Center bombing,

467
00:34:45,381 --> 00:34:52,100
My detachment was alerted to deploy to Afghanistan to conduct unconventional warfare operations.

468
00:34:52,840 --> 00:34:59,980
By November 2001, Captain Amarin and his Green Berets were fighting alongside Northern Alliance leader Hamid Karzai.

469
00:35:03,240 --> 00:35:08,360
They relied on precision airstrikes to hold their own against overwhelming Taliban forces.

470
00:35:08,361 --> 00:35:16,200
And when we described our location to the aircraft overhead, the response was, that's all you've got.

471
00:35:17,360 --> 00:35:22,520
And the pilots, to their credit, knew exactly how dire the situation was.

472
00:35:23,100 --> 00:35:27,720
And before long, the sky was just loaded with F-18s and F-14s were coming to our aid.

473
00:35:28,220 --> 00:35:33,680
And they bombed the hell out of that Taliban convoy all morning as we directed the airstrikes.

474
00:35:33,681 --> 00:35:40,820
On December 5th, the little guerrilla unit had advanced closer to Kandahar.

475
00:35:42,240 --> 00:35:45,180
Karzai had just been named interim president of Afghanistan.

476
00:35:46,140 --> 00:35:49,580
He was expecting to receive surrender terms from Kandahar at any moment.

477
00:35:51,360 --> 00:35:56,060
A separate army command unit was directing airstrikes against some Taliban diehards.

478
00:35:57,380 --> 00:36:02,500
From 39,000 feet, a B-52 bomber released a JDAM bomb

479
00:36:02,501 --> 00:36:06,440
that was a direct hit on Captain Amerine's position.

480
00:36:07,800 --> 00:36:10,980
When the bomb hit, it hit just out of the blue.

481
00:36:11,280 --> 00:36:15,200
There's no whistle, there's no telltale sign that a bomb's about to strike.

482
00:36:15,780 --> 00:36:19,360
All I knew is there was a massive explosion and I flew through the air.

483
00:36:20,440 --> 00:36:26,500
That morning, I lost 22 of my Afghani soldiers,

484
00:36:27,520 --> 00:36:29,580
22 of my best Afghani soldiers.

485
00:36:29,581 --> 00:36:37,600
I also lost my team sergeant, Jefferson Davis, my senior communications sergeant, Daniel Petitory.

486
00:36:38,520 --> 00:36:42,620
A friend of mine that was with the headquarters named Cody Prosser was also killed.

487
00:36:44,700 --> 00:36:48,740
Amoreen himself had both eardrums blown out and shrapnel in his leg.

488
00:36:49,680 --> 00:36:51,520
Hamid Karzai was wounded in the face.

489
00:36:53,880 --> 00:36:57,460
The preliminary report on the incident, called a fatality bulletin,

490
00:36:57,461 --> 00:37:00,680
blamed an overconfident operator who, quote,

491
00:37:01,340 --> 00:37:05,040
failed to have his assistants double-check and confirm his target calculations.

492
00:37:06,300 --> 00:37:12,020
And so you have three Americans died in the strike, and Hamid Karzai almost died.

493
00:37:12,540 --> 00:37:17,480
Now, had he been killed, where would Afghanistan be today?

494
00:37:19,220 --> 00:37:24,040
Having experienced the alpha and omega of smart bombs, what does Captain Amarim think?

495
00:37:24,041 --> 00:37:28,960
One thing I can't stress enough is things are going to go wrong.

496
00:37:29,260 --> 00:37:30,540
It's never going to be sterile.

497
00:37:30,900 --> 00:37:34,140
You're always going to be hurting people that you intend to hurt

498
00:37:34,141 --> 00:37:35,740
and also people you don't intend to hurt.

499
00:37:36,320 --> 00:37:41,160
It's just you do your best to make sure that the bombs are dropping on the right target.

500
00:37:41,820 --> 00:37:44,120
Warheads on foreheads, as a friend of mine would say.

501
00:37:45,480 --> 00:37:49,140
The 80% of direct hits means the technology works.

502
00:37:49,141 --> 00:37:57,180
the occasional accidents remind us that war will never really be surgical

503
00:37:57,181 --> 00:38:01,180
the danger in thinking that smart bombs are somehow safe

504
00:38:01,181 --> 00:38:09,240
is that we may be quicker on the trigger the threshold for going to war is lowering the more

505
00:38:09,241 --> 00:38:17,680
you use these weapons the more we see in these weapons a license to resort to war more frequently

506
00:38:17,681 --> 00:38:23,400
the more we will see that civilian casualties are not a thing of the past.

507
00:38:24,820 --> 00:38:28,360
But it's the military's job to be ready for any eventuality.

508
00:38:29,400 --> 00:38:32,680
Future wars may be fought with smart weapons like these.

509
00:38:33,460 --> 00:38:36,680
Robot paratroopers and pinwheeling killing machines.

510
00:38:41,380 --> 00:38:46,520
The average time for choosing and approving smart bomb targets in Desert Storm was 72 hours.

511
00:38:47,220 --> 00:38:50,780
By Operation Iraqi Freedom, the average was only a fraction of that

512
00:38:50,781 --> 00:38:52,940
and could be done in as little as 12 minutes.

513
00:38:53,640 --> 00:38:55,960
Smart bombs will return on Modern Marvels.

514
00:39:01,840 --> 00:39:05,660
On March 11, 2003, at Eglin Air Force Base,

515
00:39:06,080 --> 00:39:10,400
a giant weapon with the gaudy nickname Mother of All Bombs was tested.

516
00:39:11,360 --> 00:39:15,380
It is a smart bomb. It has an INS GPS kit on it.

517
00:39:15,381 --> 00:39:22,260
It can be launched from very high altitudes and therefore can attack targets very precisely.

518
00:39:22,820 --> 00:39:25,480
MOAB, or Massive Ordnance Airburst Bomb.

519
00:39:26,180 --> 00:39:34,400
About $170,000 a piece, packed with 21,000 pounds of explosives, designed in part to clear out large areas of vegetation.

520
00:39:35,660 --> 00:39:37,120
This will buzz cut your back 40.

521
00:39:37,780 --> 00:39:39,700
But it's not the future of smart bombs.

522
00:39:39,701 --> 00:39:43,340
except for its precision guidance

523
00:39:43,341 --> 00:39:46,880
MOAB is an old-fashioned weapon known as a daisy cutter

524
00:39:46,881 --> 00:39:51,540
but the trend in smart bombs is away from heavyweights like MOAB

525
00:39:51,541 --> 00:39:53,520
to smaller and lighter ordnance

526
00:39:53,521 --> 00:39:57,160
the more accurate the bomb, the less boom you need

527
00:39:57,161 --> 00:40:01,320
this is a test of the SDB or small diameter bomb

528
00:40:01,321 --> 00:40:06,600
the $100,000 plus bomb is actually a mini bunker buster

529
00:40:07,560 --> 00:40:13,460
Like its big brothers, it's guided by GPS and can penetrate more than six feet of reinforced concrete.

530
00:40:14,460 --> 00:40:17,180
But the SDB's warhead weighs a mere 50 pounds.

531
00:40:18,840 --> 00:40:24,400
Even non-explosive dummy bombs, once only used for practice, have been dropped on the enemy.

532
00:40:25,020 --> 00:40:30,540
Instead of high explosives inside the bombs, they used concrete to give it the same weight,

533
00:40:30,720 --> 00:40:35,900
and they allowed the accuracy of the paveway, the laser guidance, to actually take out the specific target

534
00:40:35,901 --> 00:40:40,760
without the explosion and without destroying anything surrounding the target.

535
00:40:43,260 --> 00:40:47,240
The other trend in precision bombing is known as joint standoff,

536
00:40:47,480 --> 00:40:52,680
giving weapons great range, allowing pilots to stand off miles from harm's way.

537
00:40:54,160 --> 00:40:57,920
The Joint Air-to-Surface Standoff Missile, or JASM,

538
00:40:58,580 --> 00:41:02,100
is just now finalizing its operational test.

539
00:41:02,101 --> 00:41:07,440
So this has not been used in operational use yet, has not seen war.

540
00:41:07,940 --> 00:41:14,320
However, it's been very successful in its testing, and this is essentially the future of our weaponry.

541
00:41:15,660 --> 00:41:21,920
Costing between $400,000 and $600,000, JASM can fly to a war zone up to 70 miles away.

542
00:41:22,760 --> 00:41:30,040
Arriving above an enemy position, it begins circling, trolling for targets while feeding TV images back to command stations.

543
00:41:30,041 --> 00:41:38,260
That allows the weapon to be loitering in the area of the target, waiting for something to occur,

544
00:41:38,780 --> 00:41:43,900
and in fact could receive commands against different targets after it's left the airplane.

545
00:41:44,520 --> 00:41:48,920
The ultimate smart bomb is one that chooses targets as well as attacks them.

546
00:41:49,900 --> 00:41:51,740
What's known as an autonomous weapon.

547
00:41:52,860 --> 00:41:55,500
Possibly they're the battlefield robots of the future.

548
00:41:55,501 --> 00:42:01,780
One autonomous system is a so-called sensor-fused weapon.

549
00:42:03,440 --> 00:42:08,320
Sensor-fused means it's packing different weapons, choosing them on the basis of what it sees.

550
00:42:10,500 --> 00:42:13,740
Parachuting from the bomb are many can-shaped objects.

551
00:42:16,500 --> 00:42:21,480
Miniature rotors help each one patrol a battlefield while sensors scan the area.

552
00:42:21,481 --> 00:42:30,360
It would look for a heat signature, it might look for a shape, it might look for a sound.

553
00:42:32,080 --> 00:42:37,040
The particular heat signature of a tank would signal the weapon to release an armor-piercing round.

554
00:42:43,120 --> 00:42:47,920
This is a live-fire test against tanks of the $300,000-plus weapon.

555
00:42:47,921 --> 00:42:52,120
For human targets, it would choose to spray a lethal hail of bomblets.

556
00:42:54,180 --> 00:42:59,400
Autonomous and standoff weapons may finally fulfill the 85-year-old goal of precision weaponry,

557
00:42:59,800 --> 00:43:02,920
to remove the pilot from the bombing equation.

558
00:43:04,440 --> 00:43:10,620
With pilotless drones and autonomous weapons, the dogfight may one day go the way of the medieval joust.

559
00:43:11,580 --> 00:43:16,280
Pilots of my generation feel like that potentially we're the last generation

560
00:43:16,281 --> 00:43:21,280
that's going to be able to serve out a full career in front-line combat aircraft that are manned.

561
00:43:27,080 --> 00:43:32,540
From the aerial torpedo, to the laser-guided bomb, to joint standoff weapons,

562
00:43:33,140 --> 00:43:35,080
safeguarding pilots has always been the goal.

563
00:43:37,940 --> 00:43:41,220
Protecting non-combatants, the humanitarian bonus.

564
00:43:41,221 --> 00:43:49,860
You know, in the Second World War, you had things such as Dresden, where entire cities were bombed.

565
00:43:51,080 --> 00:44:00,320
Or you had Hiroshima and Nagasaki, where just huge, massive amounts of civilian casualties were sustained.

566
00:44:02,380 --> 00:44:05,540
That's something that no one ever wants to happen again.

567
00:44:05,541 --> 00:44:11,260
With precision weapons, there may never be another Dresden.

568
00:44:14,980 --> 00:44:17,760
But there may be many more Baghdads.

569
00:44:22,180 --> 00:44:28,300
Bombs may be smart, but history will judge how intelligently they're used.

